ags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events","content":"The event system in nyx is powered by EventBuses. These are objects that store and notify their EventSubscribers when an event is called either internally or from another source."},"1105":{"id":"/use/events-5","page_id":"/use/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events","content":"You can see them as a more batteries-included way of handling events than using the native Node.js `EventEmitter`."},"1106":{"id":"/use/events-6","page_id":"/use/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events","content":"HTTP bots (`@nyx-discord/http`) have no gateway connection, so their client only emits `InteractionCreate`, plus\nwebhook events if you set them up. Subscribers to other client events never fire."},"1107":{"id":"/use/events-7","page_id":"/use/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events#quick-example","content":"That's it! Your bot will now log every interaction."},"1108":{"id":"/use/events-8","page_id":"/use/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events#client-bus","content":"The client is wrapped under an event bus, which you can get:"},"1109":{"id":"/use/events-9","page_id":"/use/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events#client-bus","content":"And you can directly subscribe to it:"},"1110":{"id":"/use/events-10","page_id":"/use/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events#custom-buses","content":"You can create and directly use any bus you want:"},"1111":{"id":"/use/events-11","page_id":"/use/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events#custom-buses","content":"You can check more information about bus creation in the Event Buses page."},"1112":{"id":"/use/events-12","page_id":"/use/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events#whats-next","content":"<Callout>Click on each card to go to its page.</Callout>"},"1113":{"id":"/use/events-13","page_id":"/use/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events#whats-next","content":"Learn how to create your own event bus, or use built-in ones."},"1114":{"id":"/use/events-14","page_id":"/use/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events#whats-next","content":"Learn how to create event subscribers, and the features they provide."},"1115":{"id":"/use/events/middlewares","page_id":"/use/events/middlewares","type":"page","content":"Middlewares","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","Events"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/events/middlewares"},"1116":{"id":"/use/events/middlewares-0","page_id":"/use/events/middlewares","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/middlewares#usage","content":"Usage"},"1117":{"id":"/use/events/middlewares-1","page_id":"/use/events/middlewares","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/middlewares#creation","content":"Creation"},"1118":{"id":"/use/events/middlewares-2","page_id":"/use/events/middlewares","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/middlewares#registration","content":"Registration"},"1119":{"id":"/use/events/middlewares-3","page_id":"/use/events/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/middlewares","content":"Middlewares are objects or callbacks that run before any subscriber is executed. They can do some pre-processing logic and store it in the metadata, or even prevent the subscriber from being executed."},"1120":{"id":"/use/events/middlewares-4","page_id":"/use/events/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/middlewares","content":"For general information about middlewares, you must first read the general Middlewares guide."},"1121":{"id":"/use/events/middlewares-5","page_id":"/use/events/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/middlewares#creation","content":"Either:"},"1122":{"id":"/use/events/middlewares-6","page_id":"/use/events/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/middlewares#creation","content":"Create a class that extends `AbstractEventSubscriberMiddleware` and implements the `check()` method."},"1123":{"id":"/use/events/middlewares-7","page_id":"/use/events/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/middlewares#creation","content":"Create a function that returns a `MiddlewareResponse`."},"1124":{"id":"/use/events/middlewares-8","page_id":"/use/events/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/middlewares#registration","content":"Register it to the dispatcher's `MiddlewareList`:"},"1125":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","type":"page","content":"Subscribers","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","Events"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/events/subscribers"},"1126":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-0","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/subscribers#creation","content":"Creation"},"1127":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-1","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/subscribers#creation-1","content":"Creation"},"1128":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-2","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/subscribers#subscription","content":"Subscription"},"1129":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-3","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/subscribers#priorities","content":"Priorities"},"1130":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-4","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/subscribers#subscription-notification","content":"Subscription notification"},"1131":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-5","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-meta","content":"Event Meta"},"1132":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-6","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-handling","content":"Event handling"},"1133":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-7","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/subscribers#lifetime","content":"Lifetime"},"1134":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-8","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/events/subscribers#metadata","content":"Metadata"},"1135":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-9","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers","content":"Subscribers are objects that are notified by an `EventBus` when an event they are subscribed to is emitted."},"1136":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-10","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#creation-1","content":"<Callout>You'll first need a type for your events arguments, here we'll use `MyEventsArgs`.</Callout>"},"1137":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-11","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#creation-1","content":"Either:"},"1138":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-12","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#creation-1","content":"Extend `AbstractEventSubscriber` class and implement `handleEvent()` and `event`:"},"1139":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-13","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#creation-1","content":"Use `SubscriberCallbackWrapper` to create a subscriber from a generic callback:"},"1140":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-14","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#subscription","content":"Register the subscriber to an `EventBus`:"},"1141":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-15","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#subscription","content":"If you want to create a listener for the `Client` bus, you can extend the `AbstractDJSClientSubscriber` (or `AbstractCoreClientSubscriber` for `@discordjs/core`) utility class instead, and you won't have to type out the client's event map generic. Check the Events Overview page for an example."},"1142":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-16","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#priorities","content":"The priority determines the order in which they are executed:"},"1143":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-17","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#priorities","content":"Higher priorities are executed first."},"1144":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-18","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#priorities","content":"Monitors are specifically meant for monitoring (e.g. metrics), though that's not enforced."},"1145":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-19","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#priorities","content":"`HighMonitor` is the first priority to be executed."},"1146":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-20","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#priorities","content":"`LowMonitor` is the last priority to be executed."},"1147":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-21","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#priorities","content":"The default priority is `Normal`, it's changed by overriding the `priority` property in classes. It can't be changed for callback-based subscribers."},"1148":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-22","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#subscription-notification","content":"The subscriber will be notified when its un/subscribed to an `EventBus`, and when said bus is unregistered:"},"1149":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-23","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-meta","content":"The first argument of the `handleEvent()` is an `Metadata` object, which is a `Record` that stores metadata about the event call."},"1150":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-24","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-meta","content":"Apart from the keys saved by the caller or the middleware, the dispatch meta contains:"},"1151":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-25","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-meta","content":"The execution ID, via the `TypedFields.Id` field."},"1152":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-26","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-meta","content":"The execution start date, via the `TypedFields.CreationDate` field."},"1153":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-27","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-meta","content":"The bot that called the event, via the `TypedFields.Bot` field."},"1154":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-28","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-meta","content":"The bus where the event was emitted, via the `TypedFields.EventBus` field."},"1155":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-29","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-meta","content":"Whether the event has been marked as handled by another subscriber, via the `TypedFields.EventHandled` field, which will be explained later."},"1156":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-30","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-meta","content":"To learn more about TypedFields, check the Metadata page."},"1157":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-31","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-handling","content":"By default, when a subscriber marks an event as \"handled\" with `this.setHandled(meta)`, it won't be received by the rest of the subscribers."},"1158":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-32","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-handling","content":"This is useful for events that only need to be \"executed once\", for example, replying to an `Interaction`."},"1159":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-33","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-handling","content":"However, you can make your subscriber to be always called, even when the event is handled, overriding the `ignoreHandled` property:"},"1160":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-34","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-handling","content":"Would log:"},"1161":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-35","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#event-handling","content":"Notice how the `Lowest` subscriber is not called, because it has a lower priority than the `Highest` one, which marked the event as handled, and it has `ignoreHandled = true` by default."},"1162":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-36","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#lifetime","content":"Subscribers can specify its \"lifetime\", which determines how much it lasts registered on the bus. This is similar to using Node's `EventEmitter#on()` vs `EventEmitter#once()`."},"1163":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-37","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#lifetime","content":"This can be done overriding the `lifetime` property. Currently available lifetimes are:"},"1164":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-38","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#lifetime","content":"`On` to \"permanently\" subscribe to the bus until manually unsubscribed (default)."},"1165":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-39","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#lifetime","content":"`Once` to subscribe for a single call (The `EventSubscriberMiddleware` must allow the execution)."},"1166":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-40","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#lifetime","content":"Would log:"},"1167":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-41","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#lifetime","content":"Notice how the `Once` subscriber is called only once, while the `On` subscriber is called twice (as many times as the event is emitted)."},"1168":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-42","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#metadata","content":"When extending nyx with plugins, you may want to specify extra data for them. You can do so with the `Metadata` object, saved on the subscriber."},"1169":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-43","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#metadata","content":"While subscribers internally save a `Metadata`, the return type of `#getMeta()` is a `ReadonlyMetadata`,\nmeaning that external objects can read the object, but not modify it."},"1170":{"id":"/use/events/subscribers-44","page_id":"/use/events/subscribers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/events/subscribers#metadata","content":"For a more type-safe approach, you can use `TypedFields`. Check the Metadata page for more information."},"1171":{"id":"/use/first-start","page_id":"/use/first-start","type":"page","content":"Start","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/first-start"},"1172":{"id":"/use/first-start-0","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start","content":"Getting started with nyx"},"1173":{"id":"/use/first-start-1","page_id":"/use/first-start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/first-start#first-start","content":"First Start"},"1174":{"id":"/use/first-start-2","page_id":"/use/first-start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/first-start#installing","content":"Installing"},"1175":{"id":"/use/first-start-3","page_id":"/use/first-start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/first-start#creating-a-client","content":"Creating a Client"},"1176":{"id":"/use/first-start-4","page_id":"/use/first-start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/first-start#creating-a-bot","content":"Creating a Bot"},"1177":{"id":"/use/first-start-5","page_id":"/use/first-start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/first-start#starting-the-bot","content":"Starting the Bot"},"1178":{"id":"/use/first-start-6","page_id":"/use/first-start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/first-start#next-steps","content":"Next Steps"},"1179":{"id":"/use/first-start-7","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#first-start","content":"A minimum version of Node.js 18 is required to run, though we recommend latest LTS. Although they should work, nyx is\nnot tested on other runtimes, such as Deno or Bun."},"1180":{"id":"/use/first-start-8","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#first-start","content":"We also recommend using a formatter like Prettier and a linter like ESLint or TypeScript ESLint."},"1181":{"id":"/use/first-start-9","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#creating-a-client","content":"You'll need a client, which will depend on the adapter you're using. It can be any client, as long as it's not logged in or destroyed."},"1182":{"id":"/use/first-start-10","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#creating-a-client","content":"No client needed: `HttpBot` creates it for you in the next step, from your application's ID and public key."},"1183":{"id":"/use/first-start-11","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#creating-a-bot","content":"Next up, we'll create a new instance of `Bot`. This is the main entry point of the framework, and it will handle the lifecycle\nof your bot, including loading commands, managing events, and more."},"1184":{"id":"/use/first-start-12","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#creating-a-bot","content":"<Callout>There's no global state, so you can create multiple bots and they'll all work independently with no issues!</Callout>"},"1185":{"id":"/use/first-start-13","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#creating-a-bot","content":"Create one with `Bot.create()`, which takes a function that gets passed the currently constructing bot\ninstance, and should return the options that you want for the bot."},"1186":{"id":"/use/first-start-14","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#creating-a-bot","content":"You can also instantiate with the `new` operator, but in order to allow dependency\ninjection, this constructor requires every manager on its options, including default ones."},"1187":{"id":"/use/first-start-15","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#creating-a-bot","content":"You would need to instantiate and pass every manager to it:"},"1188":{"id":"/use/first-start-16","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#creating-a-bot","content":"You'll find this pattern in other `@base` objects, where you can use either\n`new Object(depA, ...)` specifying every dependency, or `Object.create()` which uses default dependencies, and you can\noptionally inject your own to that method (while still keeping other defaults)."},"1189":{"id":"/use/first-start-17","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#starting-the-bot","content":"Finally, to start it, simply call `.start()`. This will log in the client, set up event listeners, and get everything ready:"},"1190":{"id":"/use/first-start-18","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#starting-the-bot","content":"This example uses `node:http`, but you can use any backend you want, including serverless!\nCheck out the HTTP Bots section for more details."},"1191":{"id":"/use/first-start-19","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#starting-the-bot","content":"If the start errors, it'll reject the promise, and the bot will enter in a `BotStatusEnum.Killed` state, meaning it won't\nbe able to be used anymore. You can create a new bot instance and start it again, but you cannot recover the previous one."},"1192":{"id":"/use/first-start-20","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#starting-the-bot","content":"To get a bot's status at any time, you can use `Bot#getStatus()`."},"1193":{"id":"/use/first-start-21","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#next-steps","content":"<Callout>Click on each card to learn more about that feature.</Callout>"},"1194":{"id":"/use/first-start-22","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#next-steps","content":"Learn how to create and use commands in your bot."},"1195":{"id":"/use/first-start-23","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#next-steps","content":"Learn how to subscribe to Client or custom events."},"1196":{"id":"/use/first-start-24","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#next-steps","content":"Learn how to schedule tasks to be executed at specific times."},"1197":{"id":"/use/first-start-25","page_id":"/use/first-start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/first-start#next-steps","content":"Learn how to create and use plugins to extend your bot's functionality."},"1198":{"id":"/use/http/configuration","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","type":"page","content":"Configuration","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","HTTP Bots"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/http/configuration"},"1199":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-0","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/configuration","content":"Replace the parts of the HTTP client."},"1200":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-1","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/configuration#signature-verification","content":"Signature verification"},"1201":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-2","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/configuration","content":"Every part of the client can be replaced through `clientInjections` (or `injections`, when calling `HttpNyxClient.create()` yourself):"},"1202":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-3","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/configuration","content":"| Injection  | Default                    | Description                            |\n| ---------- | -------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| `verifier` | `DefaultSignatureVerifier` | Verifies Discord's Ed25519 signatures. |","table":"table-0"},"1203":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-4","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/configuration","content":"| Injection | Default                       | Description                    |\n| --------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `rest`    | A `REST` with the bot's token | The REST client used by `api`. |","table":"table-0"},"1204":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-5","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/configuration","content":"| Injection | Default              | Description                                 |\n| --------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `api`     | An `API` over `rest` | The API passed to commands and subscribers. |","table":"table-0"},"1205":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-6","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/configuration","content":"| Injection | Default                        | Description                                                                                                                                     |\n| --------- | ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `emitter` | A `BasicAwaitableEventEmitter` | The emitter interactions and webhook events are emitted on. Its `emit()` resolves when the listeners finish, which is what `handled` waits for. |","table":"table-0"},"1206":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-7","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/configuration","content":"| Injection            | Default | Description                                                              |\n| -------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `acknowledgeTimeout` | `3_000` | How long to wait for an interaction to be acknowledged, in milliseconds. |","table":"table-0"},"1207":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-8","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/configuration","content":"| Injection         | Default   | Description                                                                      |\n| ----------------- | --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `maxTimestampAge` | `300_000` | How old (or how far in the future) a request can be before it's rejected, in ms. |","table":"table-0"},"1208":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-9","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/configuration#signature-verification","content":"The default verifier checks signatures with the Web Crypto API\n(`crypto.subtle`), so it needs no dependencies and works on Node.js, Bun, Deno and Cloudflare Workers."},"1209":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-10","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/configuration#signature-verification","content":"On runtimes without a global `crypto.subtle`, like older Node.js versions, pass one yourself:"},"1210":{"id":"/use/http/configuration-11","page_id":"/use/http/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/configuration#signature-verification","content":"Or implement  to use any other library. For example, with tweetnacl:"},"1211":{"id":"/use/http","page_id":"/use/http","type":"page","content":"HTTP Overview","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","HTTP Bots"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/http"},"1212":{"id":"/use/http-0","page_id":"/use/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http#installation","content":"Installation"},"1213":{"id":"/use/http-1","page_id":"/use/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http#installing","content":"Installing"},"1214":{"id":"/use/http-2","page_id":"/use/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http#creating-the-bot","content":"Creating the bot"},"1215":{"id":"/use/http-3","page_id":"/use/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http#mounting-the-client","content":"Mounting the client"},"1216":{"id":"/use/http-4","page_id":"/use/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http#setting-the-endpoint","content":"Setting the endpoint"},"1217":{"id":"/use/http-5","page_id":"/use/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http#whats-different-from-the-gateway","content":"What's different from the gateway"},"1218":{"id":"/use/http-6","page_id":"/use/http","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http#getting-the-bot","content":"Getting the bot"},"1219":{"id":"/use/http-7","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http","content":"The `@nyx-discord/http` package lets an `@discordjs/core` bot receive interactions\nand webhook events over HTTP, instead of through a gateway connection."},"1220":{"id":"/use/http-8","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http","content":"Discord sends each interaction as a `POST` request to your app's Interactions Endpoint URL, so a bot that only handles\ncommands and components doesn't need a long-lived connection at all. It can run on a regular server, or on serverless\nplatforms like Cloudflare Workers or Vercel."},"1221":{"id":"/use/http-9","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http","content":"The framework doesn't ship a server and never opens a port. Our client takes a request and returns a response, and you pass\nrequests to it from the server you already use: `node:http`, Express, Fastify, Hono, Next.js, Bun, Deno, Workers..."},"1222":{"id":"/use/http-10","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http#installing","content":"The package re-exports `@nyx-discord/djs-core`, so commands, events, schedules and everything else are imported from `@nyx-discord/http`. Everything in this guide that applies to `@discordjs/core` applies here too."},"1223":{"id":"/use/http-11","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http#creating-the-bot","content":"`HttpBot.create()` works like `@nyx-discord/djs-core`'s `Bot.create()`, but takes your application's ID and public key (both on the Developer Portal) instead of a client, and creates the client for you. `Bot` from `@nyx-discord/http` is `HttpBot`, so examples using `Bot.create()` or the `Bot` type work as-is:"},"1224":{"id":"/use/http-12","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http#creating-the-bot","content":"Requests are answered with `503` until `bot.start()` finishes, so nothing reaches your commands before they're registered."},"1225":{"id":"/use/http-13","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http#mounting-the-client","content":"Pass incoming requests to the client from your server. See Mounting for each backend:"},"1226":{"id":"/use/http-14","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http#setting-the-endpoint","content":"On the Developer Portal, set your app's **Interactions Endpoint URL** to the public URL of that route. Discord sends a `PING` to check it, which the client answers by itself."},"1227":{"id":"/use/http-15","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http#whats-different-from-the-gateway","content":"Only interactions (`InteractionCreate`) and webhook events are emitted. Subscribers to any other gateway event (messages, members, guilds...) never fire."},"1228":{"id":"/use/http-16","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http#whats-different-from-the-gateway","content":"Commands work unchanged: they reply through `api.interactions.reply()` and friends, as they do on the gateway. See Handling requests."},"1229":{"id":"/use/http-17","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http#whats-different-from-the-gateway","content":"Schedules run while the process is alive. On serverless platforms, triggering them (e.g. with Workers cron triggers) is up to you, see Serverless."},"1230":{"id":"/use/http-18","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http#whats-different-from-the-gateway","content":"Only the `@discordjs/core` adapter is supported, since discord.js structures need a gateway-backed `Client`."},"1231":{"id":"/use/http-19","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http#getting-the-bot","content":"Inside commands, subscribers and schedules, `TypedFields.getBot()` gets the bot from the metadata, typed so its client is the `HttpNyxClient` (a shorthand for `TypedFields.HttpBot.get(meta, true)`):"},"1232":{"id":"/use/http-20","page_id":"/use/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http#getting-the-bot","content":"`@nyx-discord/http` exports its own `TypedFields`, without `@nyx-discord/djs-core`'s `GatewayCoreBot` and `getClient()`, since HTTP bots have no gateway client."},"1233":{"id":"/use/http/mounting","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","type":"page","content":"Mounting","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","HTTP Bots"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/http/mounting"},"1234":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-0","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting","content":"Connect the client to your server or runtime."},"1235":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-1","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/mounting#nodehttp","content":"node:http"},"1236":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-2","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/mounting#express","content":"Express"},"1237":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-3","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/mounting#fastify","content":"Fastify"},"1238":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-4","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/mounting#hono","content":"Hono"},"1239":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-5","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/mounting#cloudflare-workers","content":"Cloudflare Workers"},"1240":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-6","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/mounting#bun-and-deno","content":"Bun and Deno"},"1241":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-7","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/mounting#nextjs","content":"Next.js"},"1242":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-8","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/mounting#anything-else","content":"Anything else"},"1243":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-9","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting","content":"Discord sends requests to two URLs, which you set on the Developer Portal:"},"1244":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-10","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting","content":"The **Interactions Endpoint URL**, handled by the client itself."},"1245":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-11","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting","content":"The **Webhook Events URL**, handled by `client.getWebhookEventHandler()`. It's optional, see Webhook events."},"1246":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-12","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting","content":"Both are handlers with one method, `handle()`, which takes a request and returns the response to send. The package also includes two adapters for the most common signatures:"},"1247":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-13","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting","content":"| Adapter          | Signature                                                      | Use it with          |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- |\n| `toNodeListener` | `(req: IncomingMessage, res: ServerResponse) => Promise<void>` | `node:http`, Express |","table":"table-0"},"1248":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-14","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting","content":"| Adapter          | Signature                                                                          | Use it with                                  |\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `toFetchHandler` | `(request: Request, context?: { waitUntil?(promise): void }) => Promise<Response>` | Hono, Cloudflare Workers, Bun, Deno, Next.js |","table":"table-0"},"1249":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-15","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting","content":"Both answer non-`POST` requests with `405`."},"1250":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-16","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting","content":"Discord's signature is checked against the **raw** request body. Make sure nothing parses the body before the client sees it, like `express.json()` or Fastify's default JSON parser."},"1251":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-17","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting#fastify","content":"Fastify parses JSON by default, so register a parser that keeps the body as a `Buffer`, and call `handle()` directly. Passing Fastify's request as `raw` makes it available to your handlers through `TypedFields.HttpRequest`:"},"1252":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-18","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting#hono","content":"When Hono runs on Cloudflare Workers, pass `c.executionCtx` as the second argument too, so the worker stays alive until your handlers finish."},"1253":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-19","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting#cloudflare-workers","content":"Pass the worker's `ctx` as the handler's second argument, so the worker stays alive until your handlers finish (see Serverless platforms):"},"1254":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-20","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting#cloudflare-workers","content":"On Workers, your secrets only arrive with the first request, so the client can't be created when the module loads. See Cloudflare Workers for a complete setup."},"1255":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-21","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting#bun-and-deno","content":"Both accept a default export with a `fetch` handler:"},"1256":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-22","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting#nextjs","content":"Use a route handler for each URL, passing `after()` as `waitUntil`, so the function stays alive until your handlers finish:"},"1257":{"id":"/use/http/mounting-23","page_id":"/use/http/mounting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/mounting#anything-else","content":"Call `handle()` with the raw body (a `string` or `Uint8Array`) and the two signature headers, then send back what it returns. The webhook event handler works the same way:"},"1258":{"id":"/use/http/requests","page_id":"/use/http/requests","type":"page","content":"Handling requests","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","HTTP Bots"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/http/requests"},"1259":{"id":"/use/http/requests-0","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests","content":"How the client answers Discord, and how commands reply."},"1260":{"id":"/use/http/requests-1","page_id":"/use/http/requests","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/requests#responses","content":"Responses"},"1261":{"id":"/use/http/requests-2","page_id":"/use/http/requests","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/requests#replying-to-interactions","content":"Replying to interactions"},"1262":{"id":"/use/http/requests-3","page_id":"/use/http/requests","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/requests#reading-the-request","content":"Reading the request"},"1263":{"id":"/use/http/requests-4","page_id":"/use/http/requests","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/requests#serverless-platforms","content":"Serverless platforms"},"1264":{"id":"/use/http/requests-5","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#responses","content":"Every request goes through the same checks before it reaches your bot:"},"1265":{"id":"/use/http/requests-6","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#responses","content":"| Request                       | Response |\n| ----------------------------- | -------- |\n| Before `bot.start()` finishes | `503`    |","table":"table-0"},"1266":{"id":"/use/http/requests-7","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#responses","content":"| Request                      | Response |\n| ---------------------------- | -------- |\n| Missing or invalid signature | `401`    |","table":"table-0"},"1267":{"id":"/use/http/requests-8","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#responses","content":"| Request                                           | Response |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| Timestamp older (or newer) than `maxTimestampAge` | `401`    |","table":"table-0"},"1268":{"id":"/use/http/requests-9","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#responses","content":"| Request                    | Response |\n| -------------------------- | -------- |\n| Body that isn't valid JSON | `400`    |","table":"table-0"},"1269":{"id":"/use/http/requests-10","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#responses","content":"| Request                                        | Response            |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| Interaction `PING` (Discord checking your URL) | `200` with a `PONG` |","table":"table-0"},"1270":{"id":"/use/http/requests-11","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#responses","content":"| Request               | Response           |\n| --------------------- | ------------------ |\n| Any other interaction | `202` with no body |","table":"table-0"},"1271":{"id":"/use/http/requests-12","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#responses","content":"| Request                     | Response           |\n| --------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| Webhook event or its `PING` | `204` with no body |","table":"table-0"},"1272":{"id":"/use/http/requests-13","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#responses","content":"Rejecting old timestamps protects against replayed requests: someone who captured a valid request can't send it again later. The window is 5 minutes by default, see Configuration."},"1273":{"id":"/use/http/requests-14","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#replying-to-interactions","content":"Over the gateway, commands reply by calling Discord's REST API, like `api.interactions.reply()`. Over HTTP they do exactly the same: Discord accepts interaction responses through the REST callback endpoint, as long as the HTTP request itself is answered with a `202`."},"1274":{"id":"/use/http/requests-15","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#replying-to-interactions","content":"This means commands, components and autocompletes written for `@discordjs/core` work unchanged:"},"1275":{"id":"/use/http/requests-16","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#replying-to-interactions","content":"The client doesn't answer Discord's request right away. It waits until the interaction is acknowledged (any reply, defer, update, modal or autocomplete response), until the handlers finish without acknowledging it (for example, when no command matches), or until `acknowledgeTimeout` passes (3 seconds by default), and then returns the `202`. Discord requires the first response within 3 seconds, so slow commands should defer first, exactly like on the gateway."},"1276":{"id":"/use/http/requests-17","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#reading-the-request","content":"`TypedFields.HttpRequest` gets the HTTP request an interaction or webhook event came from, from the metadata of commands, components and subscribers. Its `raw` property is the original request from your server or runtime, so you can read anything else from it, like headers:"},"1277":{"id":"/use/http/requests-18","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#reading-the-request","content":"`HttpBot` sets this up for you. If you create the `HttpNyxClient` yourself, add the field to the client event bus once the bot is created:"},"1278":{"id":"/use/http/requests-19","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#reading-the-request","content":"The client only knows which request it's emitting while its listeners are being called, so this relies on the bus creating its metadata as soon as it receives an event, before awaiting anything. nyx's default buses do. A custom bus that doesn't would see `null`."},"1279":{"id":"/use/http/requests-20","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#serverless-platforms","content":"On serverless platforms, the runtime may stop once the response is sent. Anything your command does after its first reply (follow-ups, edits, other API calls) can then be cut off."},"1280":{"id":"/use/http/requests-21","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#serverless-platforms","content":"Every result from the client has a `handled` promise, which settles once the request's handlers finish: your commands, components and subscribers. Pass it to your platform's tool for keeping work alive, like `ctx.waitUntil()` on Cloudflare Workers or `after()` on Next.js."},"1281":{"id":"/use/http/requests-22","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#serverless-platforms","content":"`toFetchHandler()` does it for you when you pass a context with `waitUntil` as its second argument, see Mounting. When calling `handle()` yourself:"},"1282":{"id":"/use/http/requests-23","page_id":"/use/http/requests","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/requests#serverless-platforms","content":"Webhook events need this the most: the client answers them right away, before any subscriber runs."},"1283":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","type":"page","content":"AWS Lambda","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","HTTP Bots","Serverless"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda"},"1284":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-0","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","content":"Run an HTTP bot on AWS Lambda with a function URL."},"1285":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-1","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#installing","content":"Installing"},"1286":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-2","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#writing-the-handler","content":"Writing the handler"},"1287":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-3","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#deploying","content":"Deploying"},"1288":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-4","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#setting-the-endpoint","content":"Setting the endpoint"},"1289":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-5","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","content":"This page assumes you've read Serverless, and have a `createBot()` function like the one there."},"1290":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-6","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","content":"Lambda has no `waitUntil`: it freezes the function as soon as the handler returns. The handler below waits for your\nhandlers to finish before returning, so Discord gets its HTTP response only after your command is done."},"1291":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-7","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","content":"Its reply still reaches Discord in time, since commands reply through the API, but keep commands short, and move slow work\n(anything after a defer that takes more than a few seconds) to a queue processed by another function."},"1292":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-8","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#writing-the-handler","content":"Lambda passes the request as an event instead of a `Request`, so call the client's `handle()` yourself. The body can\narrive base64-encoded, and the signature is checked against its exact bytes, so decode it as a buffer:"},"1293":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-9","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#deploying","content":"Deploy the function the way you prefer. Some ways to do it:"},"1294":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-10","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#deploying","content":"**By hand**: bundle with `npx esbuild src/index.ts --bundle --platform=node --target=node22 --format=esm --outfile=dist/index.mjs`,\nzip `dist/index.mjs`, and upload it on the AWS console. The handler is then `index.handler`."},"1295":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-11","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#deploying","content":"**AWS SAM**: a function with `BuildMethod: esbuild` and a\n`FunctionUrlConfig`, deployed with `sam build && sam deploy`."},"1296":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-12","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#deploying","content":"**AWS CDK**: a `NodejsFunction`, which bundles your TypeScript by itself, with `addFunctionUrl()`."},"1297":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-13","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#deploying","content":"**Continuous deployment**: run either of the above from your CI, like GitHub Actions with\n`aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials`, on every push.\nIt's a good place to deploy your commands too."},"1298":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-14","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#deploying","content":"Remember to deploy your commands whichever way you choose."},"1299":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda-15","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda#setting-the-endpoint","content":"On the Developer Portal, set your app's **Interactions Endpoint URL** to your function URL, and its Webhook Events URL\n(if you use them) to the same URL ending in `/webhook-events`."},"1300":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","type":"page","content":"Cloudflare Workers","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","HTTP Bots","Serverless"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers"},"1301":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-0","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","content":"Run an HTTP bot on Cloudflare Workers."},"1302":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-1","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#creating-the-project","content":"Creating the project"},"1303":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-2","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#writing-the-worker","content":"Writing the worker"},"1304":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-3","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#running-locally","content":"Running locally"},"1305":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-4","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#deploying","content":"Deploying"},"1306":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-5","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#setting-the-endpoint","content":"Setting the endpoint"},"1307":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-6","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","content":"This page assumes you've read Serverless, and have a `createBot()` function like the one there."},"1308":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-7","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#creating-the-project","content":"The bot doesn't need the `nodejs_compat` compatibility flag."},"1309":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-8","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#writing-the-worker","content":"Workers only give you your secrets through `env` once a request arrives, and don't allow network calls before that, so\ncreate the bot on the first request and keep the promise for the next ones. Pass the worker's `ctx` to the handler, so the\nworker stays alive until your handlers finish:"},"1310":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-9","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#running-locally","content":"Put your secrets in `.dev.vars`:"},"1311":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-10","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#running-locally","content":"This runs the worker in `workerd`, the same runtime as production. Test it without Discord,\nor expose it with a tunnel (like `cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:8787`) and set the tunnel's URL as your app's\nInteractions Endpoint URL."},"1312":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-11","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#deploying","content":"Put your secrets in a file kept out of git (it can be the same as `.dev.vars`), and pass it on the first deploy:"},"1313":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-12","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#deploying","content":"Later deploys keep the secrets, so `npx wrangler deploy` is enough. Remember to deploy your commands too."},"1314":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-13","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#deploying","content":"If it's your account's first worker, the deploy also creates your `workers.dev` subdomain, whose certificate can take a few\nminutes. Until then, requests to it fail with a TLS handshake error."},"1315":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers-14","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers#setting-the-endpoint","content":"On the Developer Portal, set your app's **Interactions Endpoint URL** to `https://<worker>.<subdomain>.workers.dev/interactions`,\nand its Webhook Events URL (if you use them) to `.../webhook-events`."},"1316":{"id":"/use/http/serverless","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","type":"page","content":"Serverless","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","HTTP Bots","Serverless"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless"},"1317":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-0","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless","content":"Run an HTTP bot on serverless platforms like Cloudflare Workers, Vercel or AWS Lambda."},"1318":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-1","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless#creating-the-bot","content":"Creating the bot"},"1319":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-2","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless#deploying-commands","content":"Deploying commands"},"1320":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-3","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless#keeping-work-alive","content":"Keeping work alive"},"1321":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-4","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless#testing-without-discord","content":"Testing without Discord"},"1322":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-5","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless#platform-examples","content":"Platform examples"},"1323":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-6","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless","content":"On serverless platforms there's no long-lived process: the platform starts an instance when a request arrives, may reuse it\nfor a while, and stops it whenever it wants. The bot works the same way there, but a few things need to change compared\nto a regular server."},"1324":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-7","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#creating-the-bot","content":"Create the bot once per instance and reuse it for every request the instance receives. Put its creation in a function,\nso your platform's entry and your command deploy script can share it:"},"1325":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-8","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#deploying-commands","content":"Each new instance runs `bot.start()`, so with `deployCommands: true`, every cold start would deploy your commands to Discord\nagain, wasting rate limits and slowing down the first request. Create the bot with `deployCommands: false` on the platform,\nand deploy commands from your machine (or CI) with a script that reuses the same function:"},"1326":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-9","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#deploying-commands","content":"Run it again whenever you add or change a command. Commands are matched by name, so the bot doesn't need to know their IDs."},"1327":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-10","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#keeping-work-alive","content":"The platform may stop the instance once the response is sent, cutting off anything your commands do after their first\nreply. Pass the platform's `waitUntil` to the client, see Serverless platforms.\nPlatforms without one, like AWS Lambda, need to wait for the handlers before responding."},"1328":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-11","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#keeping-work-alive","content":"Schedules don't run by themselves either, since there's no process to keep their timers. Trigger the work from your\nplatform's scheduled jobs instead, like Workers cron triggers."},"1329":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-12","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#testing-without-discord","content":"You can test your endpoint before setting it on the Developer Portal by signing requests yourself. Generate a keypair, use\nits public key as `DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY`, and sign each request like Discord does:"},"1330":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-13","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#testing-without-discord","content":"That's a `PING`. Sending an application command interaction runs your command, though its reply fails with\n`Unknown interaction`, since Discord never created it."},"1331":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-14","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#platform-examples","content":"<Card title=\"Cloudflare Workers\" icon=\"<SiCloudflareworkers />\" href=\"/use/http/serverless/cloudflare-workers\" external=\"false\" />"},"1332":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-15","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#platform-examples","content":"<Card title=\"Vercel\" icon=\"<SiVercel />\" href=\"/use/http/serverless/vercel\" external=\"false\" />"},"1333":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-16","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#platform-examples","content":"<Card title=\"Netlify\" icon=\"<SiNetlify />\" href=\"/use/http/serverless/netlify\" external=\"false\" />"},"1334":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-17","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#platform-examples","content":"<Card title=\"Supabase Edge Functions\" icon=\"<SiSupabase />\" href=\"/use/http/serverless/supabase\" external=\"false\" />"},"1335":{"id":"/use/http/serverless-18","page_id":"/use/http/serverless","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless#platform-examples","content":"<Card title=\"AWS Lambda\" icon=\"<LambdaIcon />\" href=\"/use/http/serverless/aws-lambda\" external=\"false\" />"},"1336":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","type":"page","content":"Netlify","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","HTTP Bots","Serverless"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify"},"1337":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-0","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","content":"Run an HTTP bot on Netlify Functions."},"1338":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-1","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#installing","content":"Installing"},"1339":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-2","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#writing-the-function","content":"Writing the function"},"1340":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-3","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#running-locally","content":"Running locally"},"1341":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-4","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#deploying","content":"Deploying"},"1342":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-5","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#setting-the-endpoint","content":"Setting the endpoint"},"1343":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-6","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","content":"This page assumes you've read Serverless, and have a `createBot()` function like the one there."},"1344":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-7","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#installing","content":"In your project, install the bot and Netlify's types:"},"1345":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-8","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#writing-the-function","content":"Functions go in `netlify/functions/`, and `config.path` sets the URLs they're served at. Your secrets are in `process.env`\nwhen the module loads, so the bot can be created right away. Netlify's `context` has a `waitUntil`, so pass it as is to keep\nthe function alive until your handlers finish:"},"1346":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-9","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#running-locally","content":"Put your secrets in `.env`:"},"1347":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-10","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#running-locally","content":"Test it without Discord at `http://localhost:8888/interactions`, or\nexpose it with `npx netlify dev --live` and set that URL as your app's Interactions Endpoint URL."},"1348":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-11","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#deploying","content":"Add your secrets to the site, from your `.env` file:"},"1349":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-12","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#deploying","content":"Remember to deploy your commands too."},"1350":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify-13","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/netlify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/netlify#setting-the-endpoint","content":"On the Developer Portal, set your app's **Interactions Endpoint URL** to `https://<site>.netlify.app/interactions`,\nand its Webhook Events URL (if you use them) to `https://<site>.netlify.app/webhook-events`."},"1351":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","type":"page","content":"Supabase Edge Functions","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","HTTP Bots","Serverless"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase"},"1352":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-0","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","content":"Run an HTTP bot on Supabase Edge Functions."},"1353":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-1","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#creating-the-function","content":"Creating the function"},"1354":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-2","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#writing-the-function","content":"Writing the function"},"1355":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-3","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#running-locally","content":"Running locally"},"1356":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-4","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#deploying","content":"Deploying"},"1357":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-5","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#setting-the-endpoint","content":"Setting the endpoint"},"1358":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-6","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","content":"This page assumes you've read Serverless, and have a `createBot()` function like the one there.\nEdge Functions run on Deno, so packages are imported with `npm:` specifiers."},"1359":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-7","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#creating-the-function","content":"In a project set up with the Supabase CLI:"},"1360":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-8","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#creating-the-function","content":"Discord doesn't send Supabase's authorization header, so turn off JWT verification for the function in `supabase/config.toml`:"},"1361":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-9","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#writing-the-function","content":"Your secrets are in `Deno.env` when the module loads, so the bot can be created right away. Pass `EdgeRuntime.waitUntil`,\nso the function stays alive until your handlers finish. Keep the `edge-runtime.d.ts` import the CLI generated, which types it:"},"1362":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-10","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#writing-the-function","content":"Import `HttpBot` in your `bot.ts` from `npm:@nyx-discord/http` as well."},"1363":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-11","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#running-locally","content":"Put your secrets in `supabase/functions/.env`:"},"1364":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-12","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#running-locally","content":"This needs Docker running. Test it without Discord at\n`http://localhost:54321/functions/v1/discord`."},"1365":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-13","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#deploying","content":"Remember to deploy your commands too, from Node or with `deno run`."},"1366":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase-14","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/supabase","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/supabase#setting-the-endpoint","content":"On the Developer Portal, set your app's **Interactions Endpoint URL** to `https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/functions/v1/discord`,\nand its Webhook Events URL (if you use them) to `.../functions/v1/discord/webhook-events`."},"1367":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","type":"page","content":"Vercel","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","HTTP Bots","Serverless"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel"},"1368":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-0","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","content":"Run an HTTP bot on Vercel Functions."},"1369":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-1","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#installing","content":"Installing"},"1370":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-2","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#writing-the-function","content":"Writing the function"},"1371":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-3","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#running-locally","content":"Running locally"},"1372":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-4","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#deploying","content":"Deploying"},"1373":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-5","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#setting-the-endpoint","content":"Setting the endpoint"},"1374":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-6","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","content":"This page assumes you've read Serverless, and have a `createBot()` function like the one there.\nIf your bot lives in a Next.js app, use a route handler instead."},"1375":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-7","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#installing","content":"In your project, install the bot and Vercel's helpers:"},"1376":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-8","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#writing-the-function","content":"Every file in `api/` is a function, served at its path. Your secrets are in `process.env` when the module loads, so the bot\ncan be created right away. Pass `waitUntil` from `@vercel/functions`, so the function stays alive until your handlers finish:"},"1377":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-9","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#writing-the-function","content":"For webhook events, add an `api/webhook-events.ts` that does the same with `toFetchHandler(bot.getClient().getWebhookEventHandler())`."},"1378":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-10","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#running-locally","content":"Put your secrets in `.env.local`:"},"1379":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-11","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#running-locally","content":"Test it without Discord at `http://localhost:3000/api/interactions`, or\nexpose it with a tunnel and set the tunnel's URL as your app's Interactions Endpoint URL."},"1380":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-12","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#deploying","content":"Add your secrets to the project, for each of `APP_ID`, `DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY` and `TOKEN`:"},"1381":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-13","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#deploying","content":"Remember to deploy your commands too."},"1382":{"id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel-14","page_id":"/use/http/serverless/vercel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/serverless/vercel#setting-the-endpoint","content":"On the Developer Portal, set your app's **Interactions Endpoint URL** to `https://<project>.vercel.app/api/interactions`,\nand its Webhook Events URL (if you use them) to `https://<project>.vercel.app/api/webhook-events`."},"1383":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","type":"page","content":"Webhook events","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","HTTP Bots"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/http/webhook-events"},"1384":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events-0","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/webhook-events","content":"Receive app authorizations, entitlements and other webhook events."},"1385":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events-1","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/webhook-events#mounting","content":"Mounting"},"1386":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events-2","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/http/webhook-events#subscribing","content":"Subscribing"},"1387":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events-3","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/webhook-events","content":"Besides interactions, Discord can send some events about your app over HTTP: webhook events. For example, when a user authorizes or deauthorizes your app, or when an entitlement is created."},"1388":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events-4","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/webhook-events","content":"They're sent to a separate URL, the **Webhook Events URL**, which you set on the Developer Portal under **Webhooks**, along with the events you want to receive."},"1389":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events-5","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/webhook-events#mounting","content":"`client.getWebhookEventHandler()` returns a handler for that URL, which works with the same adapters as the client:"},"1390":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events-6","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/webhook-events#mounting","content":"The handler verifies requests like the client does, and answers every event (and Discord's `PING`) with a `204`."},"1391":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events-7","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/webhook-events#subscribing","content":"Each event is emitted on the client's emitter under its type, one of the `ApplicationWebhookEventType` values. An `HttpBot`'s client event bus accepts them, so write an `AbstractWebhookEventSubscriber` and subscribe it like any other client subscriber:"},"1392":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events-8","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/webhook-events#subscribing","content":"Since they're client subscribers, the event loader picks them up too, and their metadata has the same fields as other client events, like `TypedFields.HttpRequest`."},"1393":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events-9","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/webhook-events#subscribing","content":"The payload is the usual `{ data, api, shardId }`, where `data` is the event's data (`APIWebhookEvent...Data` from `discord-api-types`), plus the event's `timestamp`. Webhook events aren't realtime and can arrive late or out of order, so use the timestamp when order matters."},"1394":{"id":"/use/http/webhook-events-10","page_id":"/use/http/webhook-events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/http/webhook-events#subscribing","content":"Discord retries an event for up to 10 minutes if your endpoint doesn't answer, and stops sending events if it fails too often. Since the handler answers as soon as the event is emitted, slow subscribers don't delay the response."},"1395":{"id":"/use","page_id":"/use","type":"page","content":"Introduction","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use"],"tags":[],"url":"/use"},"1396":{"id":"/use-0","page_id":"/use","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use#what-is-nyx","content":"What is nyx?"},"1397":{"id":"/use-1","page_id":"/use","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use#key-features","content":"Key Features"},"1398":{"id":"/use-2","page_id":"/use","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use#community-and-contribution","content":"Community and Contribution"},"1399":{"id":"/use-3","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use","content":"Hello there 👋! Welcome to official guide for using nyx, a TypeScript bot framework for Discord."},"1400":{"id":"/use-4","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use","content":"Here, you'll discover what nyx is, how to create your own bot and how to customize it."},"1401":{"id":"/use-5","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#what-is-nyx","content":"Nyx is a framework to build Discord bots in TypeScript, with adapters for discord.js,\n@discordjs/core and HTTP-only bots with `@nyx-discord/http`,\nbuilt on top of `@discordjs/core`."},"1402":{"id":"/use-6","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#what-is-nyx","content":"The main ideas behind the framework are:"},"1403":{"id":"/use-7","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#what-is-nyx","content":"Every single internal component is documented and can be easily swapped by your own. We want you to know how your\nbot fully works."},"1404":{"id":"/use-8","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#what-is-nyx","content":"As little runtime dependencies as possible, which you can also replace."},"1405":{"id":"/use-9","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#what-is-nyx","content":"Nyx doesn't enforce any specific way to do things. Choose your own folder structure, way to load commands/events,\ndatabases, naming conventions, etc."},"1406":{"id":"/use-10","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#what-is-nyx","content":"Hacky code is avoided, like changing Discord.js typings or internals.\nType safety, error handling and extensibility are prioritized over convenience and magic."},"1407":{"id":"/use-11","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#what-is-nyx","content":"Use the objects you already know from your Discord library without needing to learn new wrappers."},"1408":{"id":"/use-12","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#what-is-nyx","content":"Nyx doesn't own your bot's logic, build step or runtime. It's the layer that connects Discord to your design, so\nyour core logic stays independent and testable."},"1409":{"id":"/use-13","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#what-is-nyx","content":"We assume you have some level of understanding of TypeScript."},"1410":{"id":"/use-14","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#what-is-nyx","content":"The framework doesn't come with any commands, events or filters (like cooldowns). To avoid being too opinionated,\n  we only ship the things required for you to be able to build such features. Our optional packages provide some utilities,\n  but you can also build your own."},"1411":{"id":"/use-15","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"**Commands**, for Discord application commands. Includes support for context menu commands, subcommands, and more."},"1412":{"id":"/use-16","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"**Events**, for subscribing to events, either from Discord.js or your own."},"1413":{"id":"/use-17","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"**Schedules**, for timed tasks."},"1414":{"id":"/use-18","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"**Plugins**, for pieces that extend nyx."},"1415":{"id":"/use-19","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"All of these features are documented in detail in their respective sections, which we'll cover next."},"1416":{"id":"/use-20","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"Through optional packages, it also supports:"},"1417":{"id":"/use-21","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"**Sessions**, for stateful interaction conversations, similar to collectors."},"1418":{"id":"/use-22","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"**Loaders**, for scanning directories to load commands, events, and schedules."},"1419":{"id":"/use-23","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"**Type-safe Options**, for creating commands with type-safe options."},"1420":{"id":"/use-24","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"The codeblocks in this guide are powered by Twoslash, meaning:"},"1421":{"id":"/use-25","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"You can hover over variables, functions, etc. to see their documentation."},"1422":{"id":"/use-26","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#key-features","content":"Every example (only often skipping trivial or already mentioned declarations) will work, otherwise this guide wouldn't compile!"},"1423":{"id":"/use-27","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#community-and-contribution","content":"<Callout>Click on each card to go to its page.</Callout>"},"1424":{"id":"/use-28","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#community-and-contribution","content":"Join our Discord server to ask questions, share your creations, and get help\nfrom the team or the community."},"1425":{"id":"/use-29","page_id":"/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use#community-and-contribution","content":"If you want to contribute, you can do so by opening issues or pull requests\non our GitHub repository."},"1426":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","type":"page","content":"Error Handling","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","Other"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/other/error-handling"},"1427":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-0","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/other/error-handling#manipulating-consumers","content":"Manipulating consumers"},"1428":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-1","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/other/error-handling#consumer-resolution","content":"Consumer resolution"},"1429":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-2","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/error-handling","content":"Every executor holds an `ErrorHandler`, where errors thrown by middlewares and executed objects are handled."},"1430":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-3","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/error-handling","content":"It holds a map of consumers, where each handle a specific error class. It also receives the erroring object, and the arguments that were used to call it."},"1431":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-4","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/error-handling#manipulating-consumers","content":"<Callout>This example will use the `CommandManager`'s handler, but it works the same for other executors.</Callout>"},"1432":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-5","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/error-handling#manipulating-consumers","content":"To add a consumer, use `ErrorHandler#setConsumer()`:"},"1433":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-6","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/error-handling#manipulating-consumers","content":"You can also remove consumers:"},"1434":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-7","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/error-handling#manipulating-consumers","content":"And set a fallback consumer, for all errors that don't have a consumer:"},"1435":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-8","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/error-handling#consumer-resolution","content":"The error handler resolves a consumer based on the error prototype, going from the top to the bottom."},"1436":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-9","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/error-handling#consumer-resolution","content":"For instance:"},"1437":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-10","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/error-handling#consumer-resolution","content":"This will log `MyError` when an instance of `MyError2` is thrown, because it's a subclass."},"1438":{"id":"/use/other/error-handling-11","page_id":"/use/other/error-handling","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/error-handling#consumer-resolution","content":"If no consumer is found, the fallback consumer is used, whose error type is simply `object`."},"1439":{"id":"/use/other/filters","page_id":"/use/other/filters","type":"page","content":"Filters","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","Other"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/other/filters"},"1440":{"id":"/use/other/filters-0","page_id":"/use/other/filters","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/other/filters#wrappingmerging-filters","content":"Wrapping/Merging Filters"},"1441":{"id":"/use/other/filters-1","page_id":"/use/other/filters","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/other/filters#andfilter","content":"AndFilter"},"1442":{"id":"/use/other/filters-2","page_id":"/use/other/filters","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/other/filters#orfilter","content":"OrFilter"},"1443":{"id":"/use/other/filters-3","page_id":"/use/other/filters","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/other/filters#notfilter","content":"NotFilter"},"1444":{"id":"/use/other/filters-4","page_id":"/use/other/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/filters","content":"Filters are objects or callbacks that can restrict the execution of a given component."},"1445":{"id":"/use/other/filters-5","page_id":"/use/other/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/filters","content":"They receive all the information about the execution (context, arguments, metadata), and return a boolean indicating whether the execution should proceed (`true`) or strictly stop (`false`)."},"1446":{"id":"/use/other/filters-6","page_id":"/use/other/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/filters#wrappingmerging-filters","content":"The framework has some utility filters that wrap or merge others, allowing you to create complex filters by combining simpler ones."},"1447":{"id":"/use/other/filters-7","page_id":"/use/other/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/filters#wrappingmerging-filters","content":"<Callout>These wrappers work \"synchronously\", meaning that it'll wait for each filter, in the order they were passed, before checking whether to proceed to the next.</Callout>"},"1448":{"id":"/use/other/filters-8","page_id":"/use/other/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/filters#andfilter","content":"Combines multiple filters and only allows the execution if **all** filters return `true`."},"1449":{"id":"/use/other/filters-9","page_id":"/use/other/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/filters#orfilter","content":"Combines multiple filters and allows the execution if **at least one** filter returns `true`."},"1450":{"id":"/use/other/filters-10","page_id":"/use/other/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/filters#notfilter","content":"Inverts the result of a single filter, allowing the execution if the filter returns `false`."},"1451":{"id":"/use/other/metadata","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","type":"page","content":"Metadata","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","Other"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/other/metadata"},"1452":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-0","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/other/metadata#type-safety","content":"Type Safety"},"1453":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-1","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/other/metadata#object-metadata","content":"Object Metadata"},"1454":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-2","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/other/metadata#execution-metadata","content":"Execution Metadata"},"1455":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-3","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/metadata","content":"Many parts of the framework introduce the concept of a `Metadata`, which can store metadata about a given object, or about a given execution."},"1456":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-4","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/metadata#type-safety","content":"Since a metadata object is just a record of unknown values, it's inherently unsafe by itself."},"1457":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-5","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/metadata#type-safety","content":"For that reason, nyx uses the `typed-field` package, which creates a unique key, and only lets you set/get with a given type."},"1458":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-6","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/metadata#type-safety","content":"The `typed-field` package is re-exported in `TypedFields.create()` method, so you don't need to install it separately."},"1459":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-7","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/metadata#object-metadata","content":"Most nyx objects implement a `getMeta(): ReadonlyMetadata` method. When creating your class, you can fill this with your own metadata, which can then be read by plugins or other entities to extract extra information about your object."},"1460":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-8","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/metadata#object-metadata","content":"As a plugin developer, you may also choose to use the `TypedFields` API to create and expose a field:"},"1461":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-9","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/metadata#object-metadata","content":"Then, a consumer of your plugin can use it to decorate their objects' metadata:"},"1462":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-10","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/metadata#object-metadata","content":"Which then you'll be able to read with `MyCommand#getMeta()` and your field, safely."},"1463":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-11","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/metadata#execution-metadata","content":"Every \"executable\" object includes in its arguments an instance of a `Metadata`, which is passed around to middlewares and filters, so they can share data between them, and to your executed object."},"1464":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-12","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/metadata#execution-metadata","content":"Additionally, the `TypedFields` class contains constant fields that the framework uses to store framework-specific data."},"1465":{"id":"/use/other/metadata-13","page_id":"/use/other/metadata","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/metadata#execution-metadata","content":"Forcing these reads is safe for metadatas created inside the framework, but not for user-created metadatas, including those created by external `EventBuses` (since they're bot-independent)."},"1466":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","type":"page","content":"Middlewares","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","Other"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/other/middlewares"},"1467":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-0","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/other/middlewares#priorities","content":"Priorities"},"1468":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-1","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/other/middlewares#responses","content":"Responses"},"1469":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-2","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares","content":"Middlewares are objects or callbacks that run before an execution. They can do some pre-processing logic and store it in the metadata, or even prevent the execution."},"1470":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-3","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares","content":"We will review some concepts before going into the usage."},"1471":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-4","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#priorities","content":"The  determines the order in which they are executed:"},"1472":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-5","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#priorities","content":"Higher priorities are executed first."},"1473":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-6","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#priorities","content":"Monitors are specifically meant for monitoring (e.g. metrics), though that's not enforced."},"1474":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-7","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#priorities","content":"`HighMonitor` is the first priority to be executed."},"1475":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-8","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#priorities","content":"`LowMonitor` is the last priority to be executed."},"1476":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-9","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#priorities","content":"The default priority is `Normal`, it's changed by overriding the `priority` property in classes. It can't be changed for callback-based middlewares."},"1477":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-10","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#responses","content":"A middleware returns a  that indicates whether it passes, forces to pass, or fails. If a middleware fails, the execution will stop."},"1478":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-11","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#responses","content":"Use the utility methods from the abstract middleware class to create the response:"},"1479":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-12","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#responses","content":"`true()` - The middleware passes, and the execution will continue."},"1480":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-13","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#responses","content":"`false()` - The middleware fails, and the execution will stop."},"1481":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-14","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#responses","content":"`forceTrue()` - The middleware forces to pass, even if other later middlewares would fail."},"1482":{"id":"/use/other/middlewares-15","page_id":"/use/other/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/other/middlewares#responses","content":"If you're using a callback-based middleware, you'll need to create your response manually."},"1483":{"id":"/use/packages","page_id":"/use/packages","type":"page","content":"Packages","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/packages"},"1484":{"id":"/use/packages-0","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages","content":"Explore the packages that make up nyx"},"1485":{"id":"/use/packages-1","page_id":"/use/packages","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/packages#which-package-do-i-need","content":"Which package do I need?"},"1486":{"id":"/use/packages-2","page_id":"/use/packages","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/packages#interfaces-and-implementations","content":"Interfaces and implementations"},"1487":{"id":"/use/packages-3","page_id":"/use/packages","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/packages#building-something-for-nyx","content":"Building something for nyx"},"1488":{"id":"/use/packages-4","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#which-package-do-i-need","content":"You only need to install **one**: the adapter that matches how your bot connects to Discord."},"1489":{"id":"/use/packages-5","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#which-package-do-i-need","content":"| If your bot...  | Install            |\n| --------------- | ------------------ |\n| Uses discord.js | `@nyx-discord/djs` |","table":"table-0"},"1490":{"id":"/use/packages-6","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#which-package-do-i-need","content":"| If your bot...       | Install                 |\n| -------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| Uses @discordjs/core | `@nyx-discord/djs-core` |","table":"table-0"},"1491":{"id":"/use/packages-7","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#which-package-do-i-need","content":"| If your bot...                                                                    | Install             |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| Only handles HTTP payloads instead of the gateway (e.g. on a serverless platform) | `@nyx-discord/http` |","table":"table-0"},"1492":{"id":"/use/packages-8","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#which-package-do-i-need","content":"Under the hood, every adapter is built over `@nyx-discord/base`, which has the interfaces every component follows,\nplus some default implementations of them. The adapters add the missing parts that connect to your Discord library."},"1493":{"id":"/use/packages-9","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#which-package-do-i-need","content":"Every component depends on interfaces rather than on the default implementations, so you can replace any of them with\nyour own. You can later check the Extend section for how."},"1494":{"id":"/use/packages-10","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#interfaces-and-implementations","content":"`@nyx-discord/base` contains two kinds of objects, which you can tell apart by their names."},"1495":{"id":"/use/packages-11","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#interfaces-and-implementations","content":"**Interfaces** have no prefix, like  or . They describe what a component does, and are what the rest of the framework expects."},"1496":{"id":"/use/packages-12","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#interfaces-and-implementations","content":"**Implementations** have a prefix that tells you how to use them:"},"1497":{"id":"/use/packages-13","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#interfaces-and-implementations","content":"| Prefix     | Meaning                                                                                                        | Example |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `Abstract` | Needs to be extended to be used. You'll extend these to create your own commands, subscribers, schedules, etc. |         |","table":"table-1"},"1498":{"id":"/use/packages-14","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#interfaces-and-implementations","content":"| Prefix  | Meaning                                                                           | Example |\n| ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `Basic` | A complete, simple implementation. Use it directly, or extend it to customize it. |         |","table":"table-1"},"1499":{"id":"/use/packages-15","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#interfaces-and-implementations","content":"| Prefix    | Meaning                                                                                         | Example |\n| --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `Default` | The implementation the bot uses internally. To customize it, extend it and inject your version. |         |","table":"table-1"},"1500":{"id":"/use/packages-16","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#interfaces-and-implementations","content":"| Prefix | Meaning                                                                                         | Example |\n| ------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `Base` | A Discord-library-agnostic foundation that the adapters build on. You won't use these directly. |         |","table":"table-1"},"1501":{"id":"/use/packages-17","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#interfaces-and-implementations","content":"For example, your adapter's  builds on , adding the types of your Discord library. Extend the adapter's version, not the `Base` one."},"1502":{"id":"/use/packages-18","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#interfaces-and-implementations","content":"When you want to specify a type, prefer the interfaces, e.g. `consume(cmd: SubCommand)` instead\nof `consume(cmd: AbstractSubCommand)`. That way your code works with any implementation, following\nDependency inversion."},"1503":{"id":"/use/packages-19","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#building-something-for-nyx","content":"If you're developing something for nyx itself, such as a plugin, you can depend on `@nyx-discord/base` instead of an\nadapter, so your component works across all of them."},"1504":{"id":"/use/packages-20","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#building-something-for-nyx","content":"`@nyx-discord/base` also lets you import each kind of object separately:"},"1505":{"id":"/use/packages-21","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#building-something-for-nyx","content":"| Import              | Contains    |\n| ------------------- | ----------- |\n| `@nyx-discord/base` | Everything. |","table":"table-2"},"1506":{"id":"/use/packages-22","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#building-something-for-nyx","content":"| Import                    | Contains             |\n| ------------------------- | -------------------- |\n| `@nyx-discord/base/types` | Only the interfaces. |","table":"table-2"},"1507":{"id":"/use/packages-23","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#building-something-for-nyx","content":"| Import                       | Contains                  |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `@nyx-discord/base/defaults` | Only the implementations. |","table":"table-2"},"1508":{"id":"/use/packages-24","page_id":"/use/packages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/packages#building-something-for-nyx","content":"Importing your types from `@nyx-discord/base/types` makes it impossible to accidentally depend on an implementation."},"1509":{"id":"/use/plugins/events","page_id":"/use/plugins/events","type":"page","content":"Events","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","Plugins"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/plugins/events"},"1510":{"id":"/use/plugins/events-0","page_id":"/use/plugins/events","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/plugins/events#events","content":"Events"},"1511":{"id":"/use/plugins/events-1","page_id":"/use/plugins/events","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/plugins/events#usage","content":"Usage"},"1512":{"id":"/use/plugins/events-2","page_id":"/use/plugins/events","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/plugins/events#subscriber-creation","content":"Subscriber creation"},"1513":{"id":"/use/plugins/events-3","page_id":"/use/plugins/events","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/plugins/events#subscription","content":"Subscription"},"1514":{"id":"/use/plugins/events-4","page_id":"/use/plugins/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/plugins/events","content":"<Callout type=\"warn\">It's recommended to read the Events Overview and Event Subscribers pages first.</Callout>"},"1515":{"id":"/use/plugins/events-5","page_id":"/use/plugins/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/plugins/events","content":"The `PluginManager` has an `EventBus` that allows you to listen to events related to plugins."},"1516":{"id":"/use/plugins/events-6","page_id":"/use/plugins/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/plugins/events","content":"This is useful for monitoring, logging, or any other custom logic you want to execute when a plugin is added/removed."},"1517":{"id":"/use/plugins/events-7","page_id":"/use/plugins/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/plugins/events#events","content":"The following events are available:"},"1518":{"id":"/use/plugins/events-8","page_id":"/use/plugins/events","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/plugins/events#subscriber-creation","content":"Create a subscriber that will listen to a given event, use the `PluginEventArgs` type for the arguments map. Check t