tion about the schedule and the execution metadata, and return a boolean indicating whether the schedule should be executed or not."},"1550":{"id":"/use/schedules/filters-10","page_id":"/use/schedules/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/filters","content":"For more general information about filters, you must first read the general Filters guide."},"1551":{"id":"/use/schedules/filters-11","page_id":"/use/schedules/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/filters#creation","content":"Either:"},"1552":{"id":"/use/schedules/filters-12","page_id":"/use/schedules/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/filters#creation","content":"Create a class that extends `AbstractScheduleFilter` and implements the `check()` method."},"1553":{"id":"/use/schedules/filters-13","page_id":"/use/schedules/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/filters#creation","content":"Create a function that returns a boolean or a Promise that resolves to a boolean."},"1554":{"id":"/use/schedules/filters-14","page_id":"/use/schedules/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/filters#filter-usage","content":"Override the `filter` property in your schedule:"},"1555":{"id":"/use/schedules/filters-15","page_id":"/use/schedules/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/filters#filter-usage","content":"If you created a class, instantiate it:"},"1556":{"id":"/use/schedules/filters-16","page_id":"/use/schedules/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/filters#filter-usage","content":"If you created a function, assign it directly:"},"1557":{"id":"/use/schedules/filters-17","page_id":"/use/schedules/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/filters#filter-usage","content":"Or use a simple arrow function directly:"},"1558":{"id":"/use/schedules/filters-18","page_id":"/use/schedules/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/filters#maintenance-filter","content":"A filter that checks if the system is in maintenance mode, and prevents the schedule from running if it is."},"1559":{"id":"/use/schedules/filters-19","page_id":"/use/schedules/filters","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/filters#production-filter","content":"A filter that restricts the execution to the production environment."},"1560":{"id":"/use/schedules","page_id":"/use/schedules","type":"page","content":"Schedules Overview","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","Schedules"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/schedules"},"1561":{"id":"/use/schedules-0","page_id":"/use/schedules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/schedules#quick-example","content":"Quick example"},"1562":{"id":"/use/schedules-1","page_id":"/use/schedules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/schedules#schedule-job","content":"Schedule Job"},"1563":{"id":"/use/schedules-2","page_id":"/use/schedules","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/schedules#metadata","content":"Metadata"},"1564":{"id":"/use/schedules-3","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules","content":"`Schedules` are objects that are ticked some time in the future, and can be used as a feature complete way of scheduling events in your bot, apart from Node's `setInterval()` API."},"1565":{"id":"/use/schedules-4","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules","content":"In essence, a `Schedule` object contains its ticking logic and its interval of when it wishes to be executed, which can be in cron format, in an amount of ms (`number`), or as a `Date`."},"1566":{"id":"/use/schedules-5","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules#quick-example","content":"Let's create a schedule that updates the activity with the member count every half hour:"},"1567":{"id":"/use/schedules-6","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules#quick-example","content":"Presences are set through the gateway connection, so HTTP bots can't set them. Schedules themselves work the same as\nwith `@discordjs/core`, as long as the process stays alive."},"1568":{"id":"/use/schedules-7","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules#quick-example","content":"Given it uses the provided metadata, it'll work even if the same instance is used across multiple bots."},"1569":{"id":"/use/schedules-8","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules#schedule-job","content":"When a schedule is added, a job will be created for it, which is responsible for notifying the framework when it is time to run it.\nIt's a wrapper over a more \"lower-level\" component that will actually do the ticking, like a cron job, a timer, etc."},"1570":{"id":"/use/schedules-9","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules#schedule-job","content":"How the job is created is entirely customizable.\nBy default, cron strings and `Date`s use the croner npm package, and millisecond intervals use a timer with millisecond precision."},"1571":{"id":"/use/schedules-10","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules#schedule-job","content":"There are multiple ways to get it, but given a schedule itself does not depend on a bot, you'll always need the bot:"},"1572":{"id":"/use/schedules-11","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules#schedule-job","content":"It's the entry point for interacting with the schedule's ticking logic, and can be used to pause or resume it externally."},"1573":{"id":"/use/schedules-12","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules#metadata","content":"When extending nyx with plugins, you may want to specify extra data for them. You can do so with the `Metadata` object, saved on the schedule."},"1574":{"id":"/use/schedules-13","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules#metadata","content":"While schedules internally save a `Metadata`, the return type of `#getMeta()` is a `ReadonlyMetadata`,\nmeaning that external objects can read the object, but not modify it."},"1575":{"id":"/use/schedules-14","page_id":"/use/schedules","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules#metadata","content":"For a more type-safe approach, you can use `TypedFields`. Check the Metadata page for more information."},"1576":{"id":"/use/schedules/middlewares","page_id":"/use/schedules/middlewares","type":"page","content":"Middlewares","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use","Schedules"],"tags":[],"url":"/use/schedules/middlewares"},"1577":{"id":"/use/schedules/middlewares-0","page_id":"/use/schedules/middlewares","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/schedules/middlewares#usage","content":"Usage"},"1578":{"id":"/use/schedules/middlewares-1","page_id":"/use/schedules/middlewares","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/schedules/middlewares#creation","content":"Creation"},"1579":{"id":"/use/schedules/middlewares-2","page_id":"/use/schedules/middlewares","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/use/schedules/middlewares#registration","content":"Registration"},"1580":{"id":"/use/schedules/middlewares-3","page_id":"/use/schedules/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/middlewares","content":"Middlewares are objects or callbacks that run before any schedule is executed. They can do some pre-processing logic and store it in the metadata, or even prevent the subscriber from being executed."},"1581":{"id":"/use/schedules/middlewares-4","page_id":"/use/schedules/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/middlewares","content":"For general information about middlewares, you must first read the general Middlewares guide."},"1582":{"id":"/use/schedules/middlewares-5","page_id":"/use/schedules/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/middlewares#creation","content":"Either:"},"1583":{"id":"/use/schedules/middlewares-6","page_id":"/use/schedules/middlewares","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/use/schedules/middlewares#creation","content":"Create a clas